# Cloudflare Pages reads this from the deployed root.

# The blog is disabled (see content/blog/_index.md). These URLs were published,
# so they redirect rather than 404 — a previously-live path should not become a
# dead end, and inbound links keep their value.
#
# This also serves a second purpose. Pages caches responses at its own edge with
# a 7-day s-maxage, and the zone purge API cannot reach that cache: when /blog/
# was simply deleted, the old page kept being served with no way to evict it.
# Publishing a redirect at the same path replaces the cached object instead of
# trying to delete it. Remove these three lines when the blog comes back.
/blog     /  301
/blog/    /  301
/blog/*   /  301
